Goal: Understand the molecular cause behind the mutant dumpy phenotype in Drosophila melanogaster.
Step 1: Transferring the sequences to the terminal
I already have the sequences (fasta files) stored on my device from my genetics lab work. These sequences were obtained through DNA extraction and purification.
In order to perform alignment and compare both the wild-type and mutant fruit fly DNA sequences, I must first transfer the sequences to the terminal. 
For a file named "Reference.gb," go to the Windows powershell and use the following command:
  scp "C:\Users\prasa\Downloads\Reference.gb" gprasad3@sabine.rcdc.uh.edu:/project/stuckert/gprasad3/Final_project/
I repeated the same for my mutant sequence file saved as "mutant.fasta."

Step 2: Converting the gb file to a fasta file
Since I will be using MAFFT for alignment, I need to convert my reference file from a GenBank format to the standard fasta format.
This can be done using SeqIO, which is under Biopython.
First, download Biopython if you haven't already:
  pip install --user biopython
Then, we can use SeqIO to convert the file:
  python3 -c "from Bio import SeqIO; SeqIO.convert('Reference.gb', 'genbank', 'Reference.fasta', 'fasta')"
*Note that my input file was named Reference.gb and the SeqIO command was run inside the same directory where the file was located.

Step 3: Removing hidden return characters
Since the files were uploaded to the terminal from Windows, it might contain hidden return characters that could cause errors when using MAFFT. 
These characters can be removed using dos2unix:
  dos2unix Reference.fasta
I ran the same step for my other file "mutant.fasta" as well.

